Thanks. There is one log line here that stands out.

NetworkManager:
keyfile: ipv6.dns: ignoring invalid DNS server IPv6 address '22405:100:200:72::1:17'

Notice the DNS address:

22405:100:200:72::1:17

This is invalid. A valid IPv6 address should be:

2405:100:200:72::1:17

The leading 2 has been duplicated (22405 instead of 2405).

While this is a DNS configuration issue (not the same stale node IP), it indicates there is at least one incorrect IPv6 configuration on the node that NetworkManager is reading.

I recommend checking the connection profile

Run:

nmcli connection show ens3f0

or, more specifically:

nmcli connection show ens3f0 | grep ipv6

Pay attention to:

ipv6.dns

ipv6.gateway

ipv6.addresses


The ipv6.dns field is likely misconfigured.

Also inspect the profile directly:

cat /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/ens3f0.nmconnection

(or whatever the connection file is named under /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/).

About the original CRI-O problem

From everything you've shared, I don't think the invalid DNS entry alone caused CRI-O to fail. The earlier failure was because 20-nodenet.conf contained a stale IPv6 address that didn't exist on the node.

However, since you've now found an invalid IPv6 DNS configuration, I would clean that up as well. It suggests there may have been a typo or configuration error in the node's networking.

If this happens again

If the next reboot regenerates:

CONTAINER_STREAM_ADDRESS=72.1.1.25
KUBELET_NODE_IPS=72.1.1.25,2405:100:200:72::1:25

then the issue was likely transient.

If it regenerates the wrong IPv6 address again, I would recommend opening a Red Hat support case because the generated 20-nodenet.conf is produced by OpenShift, and it should never reference an address that is not assigned to the node.

One last thing I'd like to see is the current node addresses that Kubernetes has registered. From the bastion, please run:

oc describe node sno1.lab1.ocp.lan | grep -A10 "Addresses:"

That will let us verify that Kubernetes itself has the correct IPv4 and IPv6 addresses recorded for the node.
